<p class="f52-noprint">This is the savings challenge that sneaks up on you in the best way. You start with just one dollar in week one, add a dollar more each week, and by the last week of the year you have quietly stacked up $1,378. Print it, stick it on the fridge, and check off a box every week.</p>

<div class="f52">
  <div class="f52-bar f52-noprint">
    <span style="font-size:1.35rem;color:#64748b">Check off a week as you save it. It totals $1,378 by week 52.</span>
    <button class="f52-print" onclick="window.print()">Print this tracker</button>
  </div>

  <div class="f52-sheet">
    <div class="f52-head">
      <span class="t">The 52-Week Savings Challenge</span>
      <span class="s">Save the week number in dollars, every week, for a year.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="f52-grid" id="f52grid"></div>
    <div class="f52-tot"><span class="k">Total saved by week 52</span><span class="v">$1,378</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="hmca-note"><strong>Hate the big weeks at the end?</strong> Flip it. Do the challenge in reverse, saving $52 in the cold, broke first week of January and winding down to a single dollar during the busy holidays in December. Same $1,378, easier finish. Or just save a flat $26.50 every week and land in the exact same place. The best version is the one you actually finish.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why this one works</h2>
<p>Big savings goals fail because they feel impossible on day one. This challenge does the opposite. Week one asks for a single dollar, which nobody can talk themselves out of. By the time the weekly amount gets real, saving is already a habit and the momentum carries you. That is the quiet trick here. It is not about the dollar amounts, it is about showing up 52 times in a row.</p>

<h2>Make it automatic</h2>
<p>If checking off a box feels like one more chore, let a machine do it. Set a small automatic transfer to a separate savings account, or bump a flat $26.50 a week into it and forget the escalating amounts entirely. Pair this tracker with a high-yield savings account and your $1,378 even earns a little extra while it sits there waiting for you.</p>

